{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"endowed-upset-scaup","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"# Bounded disk-ceiling architecture for the Gerrit host\n\n## Provenance / approved intent\nDiscovered from bug `cyclopean-bloodshot-hoatzin` (`3276-2f81-8c75-4ddd`): the 2026-09-02 Gerrit outage where the EC2 host's root filesystem filled, taking Gerrit offline for ~5h. Defect 2 from that incident (CloudWatch disk alarms used `treat_missing_data=notBreaching` and self-cleared when metrics stopped) stays on the cyclopean bug and was fixed/landed/applied live. This epic is Defect 1: disk consumers grow without a bounded ceiling.\n\nOperator-approved design: do not re-litigate. Implement two pillars plus one carried-over sibling:\n\n1. **Option A — per-accumulator hard size caps via native GC/retention.** Each root-volume disk consumer gets a hard upper bound enforced by its own native mechanism: Docker overlay2/image/build cache via Docker/BuildKit GC policy (`maxUsedSpace` / `--keep-storage` style caps), `/var/tmp` via host cleanup/quota/timer, journald via `SystemMaxUse`, and writable container layers via runtime storage quota or periodic reaping of exited containers. **Hard constraint:** every capped generator must also have its own CloudWatch alarm at/near its cap, so operators can see which generator is saturated instead of only seeing generic root pressure.\n2. **Option C — separate EBS volume for code-review scratch space.** Gate scratch/clones/snapshots (currently under `/tmp` by default, e.g. `/tmp/rebar-gate-snapshots`) move to a dedicated, independently-sized/quota'd EBS volume and configured scratch path, so review work cannot fill the OS/root disk.\n3. **Sibling carried from cyclopean AC5:** resolve `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly` (`3e92-b7f4-dede-4c17`) for the `/var/gerrit` data-volume prevention/runbook issue; link it into this epic rather than duplicating it.\n\nThe root volume was grown 30→60 GiB during recovery and remains 60 GiB, but that is explicitly not the fix: an unbounded consumer can fill 60 GiB as readily as 30 GiB.\n\n## Research findings grounding the decomposition\n- `infra/terraform/main.tf:27-35` already provisions the separate `/var/gerrit` data EBS volume with `prevent_destroy`; `main.tf:56-64` sizes the root block device from `var.root_volume_size_gb` and renders `user_data.sh`; `main.tf:83-87` attaches the data volume.\n- `infra/terraform/variables.tf:13-17` currently shows the root-volume size variable, and `variables.tf:19-23` shows the data-volume size variable.\n- `infra/terraform/user_data.sh:20-70` resolves the attached EBS device by volume id, formats it if needed, mounts it at `/var/gerrit`, and persists it in `/etc/fstab`; the scratch-volume story should reuse this NVMe-by-volume-id pattern rather than guessing `/dev/sd*`.\n- `infra/scripts/compose-up.sh:21-31` and `:82-99` derive Docker bind volumes under `/var/gerrit/site`; this is the current provisioning seam for persistent host-backed paths.\n- `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:233-245` documents that `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` is unset, so the snapshot store falls back to `/tmp/rebar-gate-snapshots` on the root disk.\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:149-163` resolves the snapshot store root from `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` else `tempfile.gettempdir()` and creates `<base>/rebar-gate-snapshots`; `src/rebar/review_bot/voter.py:381` clones each review into `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix=\"reviewbot-\")`; `src/rebar/review_bot/low_disk.py:69` checks free space on `tempfile.gettempdir()` before cloning.\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh:72-90` publishes data-volume and root-volume disk-used metrics; `monitoring_autodeploy.tf:207-239` declares the root disk alarm; `monitoring.tf:217-244` declares the `/var/gerrit` data disk alarm. New per-generator alarms need corresponding producer metrics.\n- `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:132-140` defines existing reactive Docker prune thresholds and keep-storage defaults; `:367-384` runs `docker builder prune` and `docker image prune`; `:410-445` triggers pressure prune on root-disk percentage. These are reactive reclaimers, not hard per-generator ceilings.\n- `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md:494-530` documents root-disk snapshot leak recovery and current janitor; `:548-569` documents manual Docker/tmp recovery; `:575-606` states clone workdirs live in system temp/root; `infra/runbooks/provision-restore.md:98-138` documents fresh provisioning order.\n- Prior recurrence tickets found: `resting-feudalist-screamer` (`2731`) July root exhaustion from autodeploy loop; `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` (`3a52`) Aug 17 root 90%; `besotted-witty-murrelet` (`e4f3`) Aug 29/30 root alarm flaps; `preodorous-temporary-piglet` (`3cef`) Sep 1 root 91–95% ENOSPC; current `cyclopean-bloodshot-hoatzin` (`3276`) Sep 2 full outage. `wakeful-ordinary-chicken` (`5a05`) is the separate `/var/gerrit` data-volume disk-fill related sibling history.\n\n## ADR draft content to land later\nPlanned ADR file during implementation: `docs/adr/0110-gerrit-disk-ceiling-architecture.md` plus marker `docs/adr/.numbers/0110`, matching the existing numbered ADR convention (`docs/adr/README.md`). No ADR file is created by this scaffold-only ticket.\n\n### ADR 0110 — Gerrit host disk-ceiling architecture\nStatus: Draft. Date: 2026-09-02. Supersedes: N/A. Relates to ADR 0005 (snapshot cache + janitor), ADR 0012 (AWS IaC foundations), ADR 0077/0069 (low-disk review behavior), and ADR 0104 (dedicated MCP ticket-store volume precedent).\n\n#### Context\nThe Gerrit/review-bot host has had repeated disk incidents, culminating in the Sep 2 root-volume outage. Previous mitigations improved reactive cleanup and alerting but did not impose hard ceilings on the generators. Root-volume growth drivers identified from the incident are orphaned Docker overlay2 layers, Docker build cache, `/var/tmp`, journald logs, writable container layers, and review gate scratch/clones/snapshots. Keeping the recovered 60 GiB root volume is useful headroom but not a correctness boundary.\n\nResearch: Kubernetes kubelet image GC uses high/low thresholds and deletes oldest unused images until usage reaches the low threshold; BuildKit supports GC with `reservedSpace`, `maxUsedSpace`, `minFreeSpace`, and per-policy filters; Nix triggers GC when store free space drops below `min-free` and stops at `max-free`; systemd-journald enforces `SystemMaxUse`/`SystemKeepFree`; systemd-tmpfiles provides age-based cleanup and btrfs qgroup-backed directory quota helpers (`q`/`Q`) where the filesystem supports them; Docker documents overlay2 image/container writable layers under `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` and `docker container prune --filter until=...` for stopped containers.\n\n#### Decision\nAdopt the approved two-pillar architecture. Pillar A: configure a hard bound for each root-volume accumulator using that generator's native GC/retention/quota mechanism, and emit a dedicated CloudWatch metric+alarm for each generator's usage relative to its cap. Pillar C: add a separate gp3 EBS volume for review gate scratch, mount it at a dedicated host path, set `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR`/temp handling so snapshot store and review clone workdirs live there, and alarm on that volume independently. Retain the 60 GiB root volume as headroom only.\n\n#### Alternatives considered\n- Keep resizing the root volume: rejected because it changes only the denominator; unbounded consumers still fill the disk.\n- One larger shared volume: rejected because review scratch can still wedge OS/root operations and per-generator attribution remains poor.\n- Root-only generic alarm: rejected because it answers only \"the disk is high,\" not which generator is saturated or whether a cap is being hit.\n\n#### Consequences\nOperators get per-generator saturation visibility and review-gate scratch is blast-radius-isolated from OS/root. Implementation must add observability producers before or with alarms, keep Terraform and host-level config provider-neutral/portable, document live apply/migration steps, and avoid directly manipulating `/var/lib/docker` internals except through Docker/BuildKit APIs.\n\n## Epic acceptance criteria\n- [ ] ADR 0110 is finalized and landed in `docs/adr/` with the next-number marker and README/index update if required by repo convention.\n- [ ] Dedicated gate-scratch EBS volume exists, is mounted at a documented path, is used by gate snapshots and review clone tempdirs, and has its own disk alarm.\n- [ ] Each root accumulator named above has an enforced cap and its own CloudWatch alarm at/near cap.\n- [ ] Existing root and data disk alarms remain present; Defect 2 missing-data behavior is not regressed.\n- [ ] `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly` is resolved or otherwise linked as a blocking sibling for epic close.\n- [ ] Runbooks describe steady-state ceilings, alarm meanings, and emergency remediation without asking operators to delete Docker internals by hand.\n- [ ] Portability criterion: the design uses Terraform plus host-level Docker/systemd configuration and metric scripts, not a GitHub-only CI trigger; validation has local/scripted fallbacks.","id":"6202-e1c7-c57f-4897","parent_id":"","priority":1,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":["infra","gerrit","disk-ceiling","operator-approved"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Bounded disk-ceiling architecture for the Gerrit host"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788374862839289515,"uuid":"67e5aff6-5925-401c-9a62-16d0b318e95d"}